# steamclient.guestpe -- declarative source list for the guest i386
# steamclient.dll: the 32-bit half of the Steam surface.
#
# WHY THIS IS A SECOND MODULE AND NOT A SECOND MACHINE OF steamclient64.
# A 32-bit Steam game does not load "steamclient64.dll".  Valve's own
# steam_api.dll asks for "steamclient.dll" -- the two names are Steam's own
# 32/64 split and both exist in a real Steam install -- and this port's guest
# loader resolves a builtin by DIRECTORY NAME: find_builtin_dll() in
# dlls/ntdll/unix/loader.c builds <build_dir>/dlls/<name>/<arch>/<name>.dll
# out of the bare name it was handed.  So the module that answers has to be
# spelled dlls/steamclient, and an i386-windows/ output of the steamclient64
# makefile would sit at a path nothing will ever look at.  The same reasoning,
# from the other end, is written up in steamclient64.guestpe's own header.
#
# There is exactly ONE copy of the sources.  SRCDIR points every SOURCE and
# GLOB line below at ../steamclient64, so the two DLLs are the same vendored
# Proton PE half compiled twice -- which is what Proton itself does (it ships
# i386-windows/lsteamclient.dll and x86_64-windows/lsteamclient.dll from one
# tree) and is why nothing under proton/ needed a 32-bit edit: the width-
# dependent parts of that code are already behind its own #ifdef __i386__.
#
# The keyword reference lives in ../steamclient64/steamclient64.guestpe.  Two
# keywords are used here that it does not use:
#
#   SRCDIR         module-relative directory that SOURCE and GLOB resolve
#                  against, and that supplies the compiler's -I paths.
#   GUEST-MACHINE  the guest machine to build for, spelled exactly as the
#                  .thunks files spell it.  Absent, a .guestpe file builds for
#                  x86_64 only; see guestpe_machine_default in tools/makedep.c.
#
# WHAT THIS DLL CAN AND CANNOT DO TODAY, stated here so nobody has to find out
# by running a game.  It loads, and its imports bind against Wine's real i386
# PE builtins.
#
# THE i386 BRIDGE HELPER NOW EXISTS [2026-08-30].  This paragraph used to say
# that every method here returned STEAMRPC_STATUS_NO_HELPER because the only
# helper was an x86-64 program and a frame is the params struct's own bytes.
# ppc64le/steamapi/helper/build-helper.sh --machine i386 now builds
# steamhelper32 from the same sources against Steam's own
# ~/.steam/sdk32/steamclient.so, ppc64le/steamtool/proton starts it beside the
# x86-64 one, and steamrpc.c reaches it through STEAM_BRIDGE_ADDR32.  The
# width magic in ../steamclient64/steamrpc_wire.h is unchanged and is now the
# backstop rather than the whole story: a crossed pair still refuses out loud
# (measured in both directions -- see the helper's README-toolchain).
#
# WHAT IS STILL IN THE WAY, so nobody reads the above as "Portal 2 works".
# [MEASURED 2026-08-30] With the helper running and this DLL actually loaded,
# Portal 2 stops saying "Steam is not running" and instead dies of an
# unhandled STATUS_INVALID_HANDLE (0xc0000008) at 7BDAFC26, on the loading
# thread, immediately after this module is mapped and BEFORE it opens a socket
# to the helper -- both helpers were still idle.  That is the next wall and it
# is in this module's load path, not in the bridge.
#
# Note also that a bare "steamclient.dll" in the registry does NOT reach this
# module from a Source title: the game ships its own real Valve
# bin/steamclient.dll and its bin/ outranks the system directory.  proton
# writes the built file's full path for that reason; the comment there has the
# measurements.

DLL      steamclient.dll
ENTRY    DllMain
DEF      steamclient.def

SRCDIR   ../steamclient64
GUEST-MACHINE i386

IMPORT   kernel32
IMPORT   msvcrt
IMPORT   ntdll
IMPORT   user32
IMPORT   ws2_32
IMPORT   advapi32

# Proton's own PE-side entry points and interface-independent glue.
SOURCE   proton/steamclient_main.c
SOURCE   proton/steamclient_generated.c
SOURCE   proton/steam_input_manual.c
SOURCE   proton/steam_matchmaking_manual.c
SOURCE   proton/steam_networking_manual.c

# This port's half of the bridge to the native Steam client.
SOURCE   steamrpc.c
SOURCE   steamrpc_generated.c
SOURCE   steamrpc_chkstk.c

# The 39 generated per-interface wrappers.
GLOB     proton/winISteam*.c
